INT. SHUTTERED CAROUSEL PAVILION - ABANDONED AMUSEMENT PARK - NIGHT

Painted horses frozen mid-gallop, their gilt chipped. A pipe organ sags in the center. The roof leaks silver light. Five figures sit on the platform's edge, boots dangling.

DUCHESS, 60s, tiny and elegant in a mink collar, taps a cane against a brass pole. MR. LAVALLE, 50s, dapper, pocket square, polishes a pair of rimless glasses. BONES, 30s, long and gaunt, sprawls across a bench seat with a thermos of coffee. SLIM POLLARD, 40s, round as a barrel, sits with a ledger on his knee. PEARL, 20s, the youngest, in a leather jacket with too many zippers, spins a lock-pick between her fingers.

DUCHESS
Gentlemen. Miss Pearl. We are assembled.

BONES
We've been assembled for an hour.

DUCHESS
And now we are in session.

LAVALLE
Is that a legal term?

DUCHESS
It's a theatrical one.

SLIM
The count is simple. Four million, in unmarked bills, locked in the carousel's center column. The man who stored it was a bad carpenter and a worse liar.

PEARL
Which column? There's six.

SLIM
The one that doesn't turn.

LAVALLE
They all turn.

SLIM
Not that one. I've watched it for three weeks.

BONES
Thank God somebody watched something.

PEARL
How do we get in?

DUCHESS
The same way everyone gets in anywhere. We pretend we belong.

LAVALLE
In a closed amusement park.

DUCHESS
In a closed amusement park, at night, with permits.

SLIM
Are the permits real?

DUCHESS
Mr. Pollard, nothing is real. That's the pleasure of the business.

BONES
Who's driving?

DUCHESS
Pearl.

PEARL
I thought I was the lock.

DUCHESS
You are both. We are a small company.

PEARL
(grinning)
Then I want a bigger cut.

DUCHESS
You'll have a bigger bow.

LAVALLE
Are we agreed on the split?

SLIM
Equal shares. Five ways.

BONES
Six.

DUCHESS
Six?

BONES
There's a sixth share in Mr. Lavalle's ledger. Somebody we've never met.

Silence. The carousel lamps flicker. Slim turns the ledger toward the light. Pearl stops spinning the pick.

DUCHESS
(very softly)
Mr. Lavalle. You told me nobody else knew.

LAVALLE
(polishing)
I told you what you needed to hear.

He puts on his glasses.
